{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787082963,"nonce":"yiiWYkCCt8q2ZF0q2kf09rCz1XY=","title":"Shrinking `digest_window_s` re-reports Cases a longer digest already counted, because the cursor is a window START rather than the instant coverage reached","message":"**What is wrong.** The digest cursor is `max(digest_window_start)` — the START of the last window a digest was sent for, not the instant coverage reached, which is that start plus the window length that was in force when it was sent. `DigestWindows` re-floors that start under the CURRENT window length and steps forward by the CURRENT length. When a policy's `digest_window_s` is narrowed, the span the old longer digest already covered is re-tiled into new, shorter windows that all sit AFTER the recorded start and are therefore all treated as uncovered. Every Case in that span is reported a second time, in several new digests, each with its own thread reply.\n\n**The demonstration.** `internal/notification/domain/digest.go:202`:\n\n```go\nfirst := d.WindowStart(lastCovered).Add(d.Window)\n```\n\n`lastCovered` comes from `lastDigestWindowSQL` (`internal/notification/repository/digest.go:155-158`), which is `SELECT max(digest_window_start)` over the policy's digest notifications. `digest_window_start` is the only thing stored — `00058` records the window's start and its ordinal, never its length — so the arithmetic above is the only place the length enters, and it enters as the NEW length.\n\nTake a policy on a one-hour window. At 13:01 it sends the digest for `[12:00, 13:00)` and `max(digest_window_start)` becomes `12:00`. An operator then narrows the policy to ten minutes, which is admissible: `DigestWindowAligned` (`:52-57`) accepts any divisor of 24 h at or above `MinDigestWindow = 5m`, and both 3600 and 600 qualify. On the next tick at 13:05, `WindowStart(12:00)` under a ten-minute window is `12:00`, so `first = 12:10`; `newest` is `WindowStart(13:05) - 10m = 12:50`; `first` is not after `newest`, so `total = (12:50 - 12:10)/10m + 1 = 5`. Five digests are emitted for `12:10`, `12:20`, `12:30`, `12:40` and `12:50` — every one of them inside the hour the 13:01 digest already summarised, and every Case in `[12:10, 13:00)` reported twice.\n\nNothing downstream catches it. `notif_digest_uniq (org_id, policy_id, digest_window_start) WHERE subject_kind = 'digest'` (00058) keys on the start, and `12:10` is a different start from `12:00`, so the idempotency index is satisfied. `WindowOrdinal` (`:137-142`) divides by the current length, so the `state_version` values differ too and the §C.7 key is distinct. Both guards are working exactly as designed against the case they were designed for — a repeated tick — and neither is a guard against a re-tiling.\n\nThe same reasoning holds in the other direction with a milder symptom: widening the window makes `WindowStart(lastCovered)` floor BACKWARD to the enclosing wide boundary, so `first` can land on a window whose earlier half was already covered by short digests. That one over-reports rather than duplicating whole windows, and it is the same missing fact.\n\n**Why it matters.** The one thing a digest promises is that it is a summary, not a stream — an operator chose it precisely so a busy namespace produces one message per window instead of one per firing. A narrowing therefore has the worst possible failure shape: the operator asks for tighter windows going forward, and gets a burst of up to `MaxDigestBackfill` = 6 messages about a span they have already read, each one a real thread reply into a real channel. It reads as oto malfunctioning, at the exact moment somebody was tuning it because it was already too noisy.\n\nIt is also silent about being wrong. The re-reported windows are ordinary digests with ordinary counts; nothing in the row, the card or the log says \"this span was covered before under a different length\". An operator reconciling counts across the boundary gets two different totals for the same hour and no way to tell which is the double.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/domain/digest.go:187-221` (`DigestWindows`, and `:202` specifically) · `:96-123` (`WindowStart`, which re-floors under the current length) · `:128-142` (`WindowOrdinal`, which also divides by the current length) · `:46-57` (`DigestWindowAligned`, which admits both lengths in the example) · `internal/notification/repository/digest.go:130-158` (`lastDigestWindowSQL`, the cursor, and the comment arguing that the digest row IS the cursor) · `internal/notification/service/digest.go` `sweepPolicy` (the `LastWindow` read that feeds `lastCovered`) · `db/migrations/00058_a_digest_is_a_window_over_a_namespace.sql` (`notif_digest_uniq`, and the absence of any stored window LENGTH).\n\n**Scope.** small to medium. The mechanism is a missing fact, and there are two honest ways to supply it. Storing the window length on the digest row — or storing the window END alongside the start — makes the cursor an instant rather than a start, and `first` becomes `max(covered_end)` re-floored forward; that is a migration and a contract change. Alternatively a change to `digest_window_s` resets the policy's coverage explicitly, so the next tick behaves like a newly enabled digest and covers exactly one window (the `lastCovered.IsZero()` arm at `:198-200` already does the right thing); that costs nothing but has to be an action at the WRITE site, and the write site does not exist in this file.\n\n**Done when.** Narrowing a policy's `digest_window_s` does not produce a digest covering a span an earlier digest already covered, and widening it does not either. A test changes a policy's window between two ticks in both directions and asserts what the second tick emits. Whichever fix lands, the fact that a stored `digest_window_start` is meaningless without the length that was in force is stated where the cursor is read, because the comment there currently argues the cursor is complete — *\"the digest row IS the cursor, so the only state that can exist is state a digest was actually sent for\"* — and that is true only while the length never changes.","files":null}]}